From d9f6fd15d84787fc4fbf2d9edc599c91351dfb3e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Film Grain Workbench <noreply@4kvcd.invalid>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 19:10:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] --fga 4: the three film grain analyses without coding

For Film Grain Workbench, which adds VVenC's film grain parameters to a
VVC encode made elsewhere. --fga 4 runs the analyzers of --fga 1, 2 and 3
on the same pictures (--fga 1 without the temporal residual, as on its
own), each with its own parameter sets, and codes nothing: pictures take
the first pass's skip path, and ALF and rate control are off (both would
wait for coded pictures; neither touches MCTF or the analysis). The
output holds, for every picture, an access unit delimiter (EncLib waits
for a non-empty access unit after each input picture), and after each
analysis, for each mode, a user data unregistered SEI (UUID
393cc818-0a4b-4cef-8e2d-f1605b9577c0; data: the mode, then the POC from
which the set applies, big-endian) followed by the FGC SEI message of
that parameter set. MCTF does only the analysis's work: motion
estimation and the analysis's filter on the first picture of each GOP,
not the encoding's filtering of the others, nor the noise levels, SCC
detection and block importance that serve the coding (the same
parameters: 960x540 960 frames faster/medium/slower, QP 17/28/40; 4K
HDR 96 frames; 1.2-2.9x faster). --fga 1 to 3 write the same streams as
before.

Also:
- SEIuserDataUnregistered frees its data with delete[], as an array.
- FGAnalyzer::mergeIntervals divided by zero when it merged two adjacent
  intervals of one intensity each (width 0, as VTM, which has the same
  division, counts it): --fga 2 crashed (0xC0000094) on The Beekeeper 4K
  HDR about 50,400 frames in, at QP 28 faster. Such intervals now weigh
  the same; everything else is unchanged (byte-identical streams where
  nothing crashed: 960x540 960 frames --fga 4, a scene-cut clip --fga 2).
